  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1233 PM CDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nAs of 12:30 PM CDT, temperatures are in the 80s with winds out of \nthe south to southwest. There is a good amount of elevated smoke in \nthe area resulting in hazy skies and some surface level smoke mainly \naround the Missouri River bringing some slight reductions in \nvisibility. There is about a 30% chance for some precipitation today \nacross northeast SD. There could be some weak thunderstorms \nproducing some small hail and/or wind gusts of 35 to 45 mph but \norganized severe storms are not expected. Timing for storms is \nbetween 5 and 10 PM CDT.\n\nA shortwave moves across the region Thursday bringing a chance for \nstorms starting Wednesday evening. There is a Marginal Risk (level 1 \nout of 5) for severe storms over Corson county, main hazard will be \nwind gusts of 60-70 mph. Precipitation chances of 40 to 50% spread \neast through the overnight and during the day Thursday, increasing \nto 70 to 90% for the afternoon and evening hours. There is a \nMarginal Risk (level 1 out of 5) for severe storms across the entire \nforecast area. The main threat with these storms will be strong wind \ngusts of at least 60 mph. There is also potential for heavy rain. \nPWAT values east of the Missouri River range from 1 to 2 inches. \nWith how dry we have been there may be a chance for some localized \nflash flooding over northeast SD but confidence is somewhat low. The \nWeather Prediction Center has highlighted areas along and east of \nthe James River in a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 4) for excessive \nrainfall. Timing for severe storm development will be dependent on \nfrontal progression.\n\nFriday through Monday looks to be generally dry. There are a couple \nslight chances for precip along the ND/SD border, but nothing about \n20%. Models are starting to show some increased chances for Tuesday \nbut that's 7 days out so few details are available. Temperatures \nthrough the rest of the work week will be a little cooler with highs \nin the 80s before we see another increase for the weekend. Then it \nlooks like we will have another slight cool down for next work week. \nWinds Sunday look to gust to 30 to 40 mph as low pressure moves in \nbehind high pressure. Gusts of 30-35 mph will continue into Monday \nas a low moves into the area.",
